ficient-stock rejection, concurrency safety, and a five-second lock bound are directly demonstrated. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the restart, changed boot identity, and recovered SSH, runtime, script, database integrity, stock, and reservation data. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Concurrency, insufficient-stock, and lock-contention behavior were exercised against an isolated database using a copy of the installed script rather than against the production database itself.","Empty, negative, and nonnumeric quantities were not each behaviorally exercised; their rejection is established primarily by direct inspection of the installed source, with zero tested directly.","The lifecycle collector does not support Alpine/OpenRC failed-service enumeration through its systemd-oriented probe, although SSH recovery and listening sockets were directly observed."]}
